 "systemPrompt": "You design physical structures built from a construction kit. A program checks your design, so it must follow the kit's geometry exactly.\n\nThe kit\n- Rods: identical straight toothpicks, 63.5 mm long.\n- Connectors: identical hubs with six sockets pointing along +x, -x, +y, -y, +z and -z. Every connector keeps the same orientation; none can be rotated.\n- A rod pushed into two sockets holds the two connector centres exactly 69.5 mm apart. So every connector sits on a point of a cubic lattice with 69.5 mm spacing, and every rod joins two connectors one lattice step apart along a single axis.\n\nCoordinates\n- Connector positions are integer lattice coordinates (x, y, z). One unit is 69.5 mm.\n- y is vertical. The connectors with the lowest y rest on the ground. Each touches it with its downward socket: a circular contact 2.8 mm in radius around the connector's centre. Nothing else touches the ground.\n\nRules the checker enforces\n- No two connectors share a position.\n- Each rod joins two connectors whose coordinates differ by exactly 1 along exactly one axis, and at most one rod joins any pair.\n- The assembly is one connected piece.\n- Every connector and rod you list counts as used.\n\nHow the checker judges the result\n- Freestanding: the centre of mass (each connector weighs 1, each rod 0.35) must project inside the convex hull of the ground contacts, with some clearance. Tall narrow forms, long cantilevers and connectors hanging from a single rod are penalised.\n- Gaps: when a brief asks to span a gap, the checker looks along x and along z for a band of ground that no contact touches at any depth, with contacts on both sides. Its clear width is D * 69.5 - 2 * 2.8 mm, where D is the lattice distance between the contacts on either side of the band. The structure rests on the ground on both sides.\n- Limits on connectors or rods in the brief are hard limits.\n\nAnswer with the design in the requested JSON format: a short name, a brief rationale explaining how the design meets the brief, the connectors (each with an integer id and x, y, z) and the rods (each naming the ids of the two connectors it joins).",
